WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.395
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.742 --> 00:00:15.120
FRANCIS LOW: Good morning.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.240
Unfortunately, I'm
not Viki Weisskopf.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.900
Viki was unable to come.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.500
He's recovering from
a medical problem.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.190
Let me assure you, he's fine.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.440
I talked to him this morning.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.700
He's getting better
and he'll be back soon.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.170
But he simply was
unable to make it today.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:40.280
I'm Francis Low, and I was
asked to share instead of him.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.620
So let me welcome
you to the morning

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.590
session of the Worlds of Philip
Morrison, in which we honor

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:53.790
and entertain, and
are entertained by,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:58.020
our friend and colleague,
Philip Morrison.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.540
Viki, 10 years
ago, wrote a piece

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.319
for Technology Review,
which I think one

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:06.360
should call an appreciation.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.220
And he was planning to read it.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.580
And it seemed to me the
best thing for me to do

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.610
was to read it to you.

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:16.410
So here is Weisskopf on
Morrison, Technology Review,

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:17.580
1976.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.960
I will change the last word.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.060
Phil Morrison is
more than a person.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.770
He represents an
attitude, a way of life,

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.350
a symbol for what I'd like to
call joy of insight or lust

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.300
for knowledge.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.150
Nobody else has better
demonstrated, or rather

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.790
embodied, what it
means to the human soul

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.660
to perceive or recognize
a new scientific discovery

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:44.480
or a new theoretical insight.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.230
I use the word soul advisedly,
because Phil's character

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.190
contains so much of it.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.780
Scientific knowledge
and understanding

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.880
is not a purely cerebral affair.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.920
It is soaked with
emotion, excitement,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.780
and nervous tension,
as everybody

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:01.186
knows who has heard Phil talk.

00:02:01.186 --> 00:02:02.560
The life of a
scientist would not

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.620
be worthwhile were it not for
those few moments, maybe two

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.380
or three per year, when
he feels an exhilarating

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.520
joy deep in his gut of
having understood something,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.700
of having seen new connections,
that bring things together.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.840
Now I know what it means.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.630
These moments of joy
occur from time to time,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.330
no matter whether it is
one's own or someone else's

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.740
discovery.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.310
In my life, more often
than not, such a moment

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.570
happened during a talk or
a conversation with Phil.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.540
I remember an unforgettable talk
at a Harvard evening seminar

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.090
more than 30 years
ago, when he told us

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.000
about Watson and
Crick's double helix

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.350
just a few weeks after
they discovered it.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.110
It was the physicist Morrison
who immediately recognized

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.720
the immense significance
of the discovery,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.690
and he gave us a broad
picture, in his inimitable way,

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:56.200
of how this development
will revolutionize biology.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.820
We went away from
it with pride to be

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.610
alive at a time of
such revelation.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.100
Phil's strength is the
broad brush painting

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.930
of the scientific landscape.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.810
You get the great connections,
the deep relations,

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.980
and the far perspective.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.590
Surely here and there
some detail gets lost.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.200
Some conclusions
are not quite tight.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.330
But these are a
small price to pay

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:23.880
for great panorama and the grand
vista which we get in exchange.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.300
Many people have asked how
Phil can manage all this.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.880
When does he read all
the books and journals?

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.070
When does he acquire
his phenomenal knowledge

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:35.490
of what happens in all
branches of natural science?

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.040
On top of all this, he has been
book editor of the Scientific

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.530
American for the
last 15 years, where

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.960
he reviews four to
six books every month,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.900
and must have read
10 times as many.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.930
He possesses the phenomenal gift
of a quick and thorough grasp.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.920
Have you ever seen
him read a book

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.660
by turning a page every
one or two seconds?

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:56.980
He knows the content.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.500
And what's more, he knows the
significance and relevance

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:00.662
of it.

00:04:00.662 --> 00:04:02.370
This fact has been
experimentally tested.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.360
I envy this in him more
than anything else.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.790
Every student at MIT
knows Phil's classic art

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.770
of lecturing-- or
should I say romantic--

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.420
I should.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.209
You can count on getting a
new and unconventional view

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:20.930
of the subject.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:24.350
Surely we must listen carefully
because the ideas come fast

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:25.340
with Phil.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.640
There are moments when his
throat muscles have a hard time

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.186
to follow the speed
of his thoughts.

00:04:31.186 --> 00:04:33.560
But these are the moments when
the lecture hall is filled

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.020
to the brim with the
intensity of his thoughts

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.730
and the tension
of his enthusiasm.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.610
If you needed proof
that true science is not

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:45.344
a dry and impersonal subject,
go and listen to Phil.

00:04:45.344 --> 00:04:47.010
He has worked in many
fields of physics,

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.600
and he is the best
counter-example

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.280
to the narrow
specialists of today.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.400
No wonder his latest
field of endeavor

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.130
is that part of science with
the widest and grandest scope--

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:58.170
astronomy.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.030
But there is more to
his human approach

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.180
to life than his broad
understanding of science.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.730
Science is the relation
of man to nature,

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:08.694
but Phil's interests go
far beyond pure science.

00:05:08.694 --> 00:05:13.020
The predicament of man, the
social and political problems,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.506
education, and health
care have always

00:05:15.506 --> 00:05:17.950
been among his most
important concerns.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.660
Everything human fascinates
him, be it great, ordinary,

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.917
or ominous.

00:05:22.917 --> 00:05:25.000
Whenever help is needed,
whenever there is a cause

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.840
to do some good, go to Phil.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.850
He will be ready and full
of youthful enthusiasm.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.240
Is he really 70 now?

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.270
Our speaker this morning
scarcely needs an introduction.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:52.852
Nevertheless, it's my
job to introduce them,

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.618
so I will say a few
words about each of them.

00:05:55.618 --> 00:05:57.880
Our first is
Professor Carl Sagan,

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.320
who is the David Duncan
Professor of Astronomy

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.770
and Space Sciences and
director of the laboratory

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.720
for planetary studies
at Cornell University.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.240
He has played a leading
role in the Mariner, Viking,

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.750
and Voyager expeditions to
the planet, for which he

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.512
received the NASA Medal
for Exceptional Scientific

00:06:14.512 --> 00:06:16.404
Achievement.

00:06:16.404 --> 00:06:20.852
His scientific research has
enhanced and [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:20.852 --> 00:06:23.590
our understanding of the
greenhouse effect on Venus,

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:27.201
dust storms on Mars, the
organic haze on Titan,

00:06:27.201 --> 00:06:31.425
the origin of life, and the
search for life elsewhere.

00:06:31.425 --> 00:06:32.800
Both of our speakers
this morning

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.136
also have had several careers.

00:06:36.136 --> 00:06:40.600
And the second career of
Professor Sagan is author.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.336
He is the author,
co-author, or editor

00:06:42.336 --> 00:06:46.056
of more than 20 books, including
Broca's Brain, Comet, Contact,

00:06:46.056 --> 00:06:47.792
and The Dragons of
Eden, for which he

00:06:47.792 --> 00:06:50.024
was awarded the Pulitzer Prize.

00:06:50.024 --> 00:06:52.504
His Emmy and Peabody award
winning television series

00:06:52.504 --> 00:06:55.976
Cosmos because the most widely
watched series in the history

00:06:55.976 --> 00:06:59.448
of American public television.

00:06:59.448 --> 00:07:03.912
There are very few scientists
who have been able to do this,

00:07:03.912 --> 00:07:06.940
to reach the public with
serious discussion of science

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.198
and what is going
on in our field,

00:07:09.198 --> 00:07:11.182
or who had even tried to do it.

00:07:11.182 --> 00:07:14.158
And I think that Professor
Sagan deserves great credit

00:07:14.158 --> 00:07:16.638
for having brought some
understanding of science

00:07:16.638 --> 00:07:19.118
to the general public.

00:07:19.118 --> 00:07:21.868
In recent years, Dr.
Sagan and his colleagues

00:07:21.868 --> 00:07:24.326
have been engaged in research
on the long-term consequences

00:07:24.326 --> 00:07:29.534
of nuclear war, and covering the
phenomenon of nuclear winter,

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:32.530
which you all know about.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.608
For this work, he was given the
annual award for Public Service

00:07:35.608 --> 00:07:37.552
at the Federation of
American Scientists,

00:07:37.552 --> 00:07:39.982
and of Physicians for
Social Responsibility,

00:07:39.982 --> 00:07:42.023
as well as the Leo
Szilard award for Physics

00:07:42.023 --> 00:07:44.356
in the Public Interest of the
American Physical Society.

00:07:44.356 --> 00:07:47.560
It's a pleasure to welcome
Professor Sagan here

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.548
to MIT to Phil's celebration.

00:07:50.548 --> 00:07:55.030
His talk today is titled
"Joining the Universe."

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.492
CARL SAGAN: Thank you.

00:08:04.492 --> 00:08:07.231
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.764
Thank you, Dr. Low.

00:08:37.764 --> 00:08:42.228
I'm delighted to be
here, to be with Ed.

00:08:42.228 --> 00:08:44.212
My voice is the voice--

00:08:44.212 --> 00:08:46.692
I think it's fair to say--

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:53.636
the world's scientific community
in celebrating Philip Morrison.

00:08:53.636 --> 00:09:02.564
I want to discuss a fairly broad
number of topics, all of which,

00:09:02.564 --> 00:09:06.036
one way or another, are
connected with Philip

00:09:06.036 --> 00:09:10.004
Morrison's manifold interests.

00:09:10.004 --> 00:09:16.948
And I'd like to begin
with a perspective, which

00:09:16.948 --> 00:09:23.892
I think Phil is one of
the inheritors today

00:09:23.892 --> 00:09:27.920
of this long tradition.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:34.846
And the tradition could shortly
be called the Copernican

00:09:34.846 --> 00:09:40.269
Perspective, but it's
much broader than that.

00:09:40.269 --> 00:09:42.487
In human affairs,
there is a kind

00:09:42.487 --> 00:09:44.706
of aristocratic
habit of thought that

00:09:44.706 --> 00:09:48.347
crops up in most
recent human societies,

00:09:48.347 --> 00:09:53.814
the idea that because of
some accident of birth,

00:09:53.814 --> 00:09:57.790
some people have an otherwise
unmerited sense of position

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:00.772
in the social universe.

00:10:00.772 --> 00:10:05.245
From Plantagenet
princelings to the children

00:10:05.245 --> 00:10:09.221
of robber barons and
central committee members,

00:10:09.221 --> 00:10:12.203
this self-serving
attitude seems as

00:10:12.203 --> 00:10:14.356
natural as breathing
for those who are

00:10:14.356 --> 00:10:16.730
born into privilege or power.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:22.078
And it's connected with
sexism, racism, nationalism,

00:10:22.078 --> 00:10:25.459
and other deadly
chauvinisms that continue

00:10:25.459 --> 00:10:27.874
to plague the human species.

00:10:27.874 --> 00:10:29.480
Uncommon strength
of character is

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:33.940
needed to resist the
blandishments of those

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:39.847
who are surest of some supposed
superiority over our fellows.

00:10:39.847 --> 00:10:43.284
Since scientists are people,
it should not surprise us.

00:10:43.284 --> 00:10:45.248
Comparable doctrines
have entered,

00:10:45.248 --> 00:10:50.170
from time to time, the
scientific world view.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:53.150
Until the 16th century, for
example, almost all scientists

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:57.263
considered it absolutely
evident from observation,

00:10:57.263 --> 00:10:59.790
that the Earth was at the
center of the universe,

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.664
and that the sun and
the moon and the planets

00:11:01.664 --> 00:11:05.087
revolved around us.

00:11:05.087 --> 00:11:08.510
And while there
were good reasons

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:13.430
on the basis of confirmation
between theory and experiment

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.405
for such a view, nevertheless,
some of its popularity,

00:11:17.405 --> 00:11:20.800
I think, must be due to a
kind of emotional resonance.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.710
The universe was
structured around us,

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:24.734
created for our benefit.

00:11:24.734 --> 00:11:25.650
We were at the center.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:29.502
Everything else goes around us.

00:11:29.502 --> 00:11:32.988
It's difficult to contemplate
such a model without feeling

00:11:32.988 --> 00:11:38.466
a small stirring of pride-- the
entire universe created for us.

00:11:38.466 --> 00:11:41.454
We must really be something.

00:11:41.454 --> 00:11:47.274
And this doctrine, supported
by common-sense observations,

00:11:47.274 --> 00:11:51.747
consistent with church
dogma, taught in schools--

00:11:51.747 --> 00:11:55.723
it survived in Europe for
at least two centuries

00:11:55.723 --> 00:11:59.202
after the time of Copernicus
before it was supplanted

00:11:59.202 --> 00:12:02.755
by the more uncomfortable
idea that the Earth went

00:12:02.755 --> 00:12:04.296
around the sun,
wasn't at the center,

00:12:04.296 --> 00:12:06.657
and it was just one
of many planets.

00:12:06.657 --> 00:12:09.639
And the appeal of this
geocentric doctrine, I think,

00:12:09.639 --> 00:12:14.112
can still be felt, and the
continuing worldwide popularity

00:12:14.112 --> 00:12:18.585
of the entirely spurious
doctrine of astrology.

00:12:18.585 --> 00:12:25.046
Well, every other such proposal
to remove the human species

00:12:25.046 --> 00:12:27.779
from the cosmic
center stage has been

00:12:27.779 --> 00:12:31.507
resisted, sometimes
violently, for reasons that

00:12:31.507 --> 00:12:33.495
are emotionally rather similar.

00:12:33.495 --> 00:12:38.530
So, consider this
list of propositions--

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:43.255
that the stars are other
suns, that the stars are

00:12:43.255 --> 00:12:46.930
made of the same kind of
stuff that we have down here,

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:50.850
that the Earth is much older
than the human species,

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:55.145
that the sun is in some
obscure, exterior spiral

00:12:55.145 --> 00:13:00.722
arm of a vast Milky Way galaxy,
that the spiral nebulae are

00:13:00.722 --> 00:13:05.815
other galaxies, that
the position, velocity,

00:13:05.815 --> 00:13:08.517
or acceleration of
the Earth in space

00:13:08.517 --> 00:13:14.006
does not give us some
privileged reference frame,

00:13:14.006 --> 00:13:17.499
that human beings, like all
the other plants and animals

00:13:17.499 --> 00:13:21.491
on Earth, have evolved from
other and more ancient species.

00:13:21.491 --> 00:13:24.235
Well, every one of
these propositions

00:13:24.235 --> 00:13:29.974
has elicited a reasonably
fierce scientific debate.

00:13:29.974 --> 00:13:33.467
In every case, I think
the furor of the debate

00:13:33.467 --> 00:13:37.958
has something more to it
than just scientific debate.

00:13:37.958 --> 00:13:39.954
It has to do with
our fear of losing

00:13:39.954 --> 00:13:43.447
our imagined importance in
the cosmos, the cosmic center

00:13:43.447 --> 00:13:44.445
stage.

00:13:44.445 --> 00:13:47.938
But still, despite the
powerful social forces

00:13:47.938 --> 00:13:50.932
arrayed against them, every
one of these hypotheses

00:13:50.932 --> 00:13:54.924
has been convincingly verified.

00:13:54.924 --> 00:13:57.419
The sequence of
deprovincializations

00:13:57.419 --> 00:14:00.912
that they represent
constitute some

00:14:00.912 --> 00:14:04.405
of the key scientific advances
since the Renaissance.

00:14:04.405 --> 00:14:06.900
These debates have
been decisively settled

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:09.894
in favor of a
proposition that can

00:14:09.894 --> 00:14:11.890
be summed up in a
single sentence-- we

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.383
are nothing special.

00:14:15.383 --> 00:14:19.375
This set of blows to the
anthropocentric world view

00:14:19.375 --> 00:14:21.371
has been more than a
little disappointing

00:14:21.371 --> 00:14:25.363
to human chauvinists.

00:14:25.363 --> 00:14:27.858
And it's hard to be
human without feeling

00:14:27.858 --> 00:14:30.353
some sense of chauvinism.

00:14:30.353 --> 00:14:33.846
But it seems to me that the
gains of our new perspective

00:14:33.846 --> 00:14:36.840
far outweigh the loses.

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:41.331
We find ourselves, maybe
trembling just a little,

00:14:41.331 --> 00:14:44.824
on the threshold of a
vast and awesome universe,

00:14:44.824 --> 00:14:48.816
rich in mystery and
promise, that utterly dwarfs

00:14:48.816 --> 00:14:52.309
in time and space
and in potential

00:14:52.309 --> 00:14:56.800
the tidy, anthropocentric
world of our ancestors.

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.794
We have sent dozens
of robot emissaries

00:14:59.794 --> 00:15:04.285
to the nearby planets and
four spacecraft to the stars.

00:15:04.285 --> 00:15:07.279
We have launched
telescopes into Earth orbit

00:15:07.279 --> 00:15:11.770
that peer billions of light
years into the depths of space.

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:16.760
And now, we are on the verge
of initiating an observational

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:20.752
test of the most recent
self-congratulatory

00:15:20.752 --> 00:15:22.249
chauvinism--

00:15:22.249 --> 00:15:24.744
the contention that in
all this great universe,

00:15:24.744 --> 00:15:28.736
of 100 billion galaxies and
a billion trillion stars,

00:15:28.736 --> 00:15:35.722
there is no species so wise, so
intelligent, so advanced as we.

00:15:35.722 --> 00:15:38.217
This topic is called
the search for

00:15:38.217 --> 00:15:42.209
extraterrestrial intelligence,
or SETI for short.

00:15:42.209 --> 00:15:49.694
And it began in a famous 1959
paper in the journal Nature,

00:15:49.694 --> 00:15:54.684
written by Philip Morrison
and Giuseppe Cocconi.

00:15:54.684 --> 00:15:58.177
These days, the revolutionary
nature of this paper

00:15:58.177 --> 00:16:00.672
is sometimes forgotten.

00:16:00.672 --> 00:16:07.658
The prevailing attitudes in 1959
were that the search for life

00:16:07.658 --> 00:16:12.648
elsewhere was somewhere between
science fiction and hokum.

00:16:12.648 --> 00:16:18.137
It had suffered serious
losses in credibility,

00:16:18.137 --> 00:16:23.127
going back to
Percival Lowell, who

00:16:23.127 --> 00:16:28.616
taught at this institution, who
believed that Mars was covered

00:16:28.616 --> 00:16:32.608
by a network of canals
built by intelligent beings,

00:16:32.608 --> 00:16:37.598
all of which turned out
to be entirely spurious.

00:16:37.598 --> 00:16:41.590
What Morrison and Cocconi
did was to ask the question,

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:48.576
what is the most effective means
of interstellar communication

00:16:48.576 --> 00:16:51.570
by another advanced
civilization to us?

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:55.562
They concluded that the
radio spectrum was by far

00:16:55.562 --> 00:17:01.550
the most convenient, the most
noise-free, the quickest,

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:06.041
compared to alternative
non-electromagnetic means.

00:17:06.041 --> 00:17:09.035
And more than
that, they proposed

00:17:09.035 --> 00:17:14.357
that in the vast microwave
spectrum, the vast radio

00:17:14.357 --> 00:17:18.017
spectrum, there were a few
specific frequencies which

00:17:18.017 --> 00:17:21.011
might be the
preferential frequencies

00:17:21.011 --> 00:17:25.003
for such communication,
because of laws of physics

00:17:25.003 --> 00:17:28.412
shared between the
transmitting and the receiving

00:17:28.412 --> 00:17:28.995
civilizations.

00:17:28.995 --> 00:17:32.488
And they explicitly pointed
to the 1420 megahertz

00:17:32.488 --> 00:17:37.478
line of neutral
monatomic hydrogen.

00:17:37.478 --> 00:17:42.468
And this is now one of the
so-called magic frequencies--

00:17:42.468 --> 00:17:45.961
that is, frequencies
which are imagined

00:17:45.961 --> 00:17:49.454
to be shared by a common
knowledge of the laws of nature

00:17:49.454 --> 00:17:51.949
and the nature of
the galaxy between

00:17:51.949 --> 00:17:54.943
a hypothetical transmitting
civilization and our own.

00:17:54.943 --> 00:17:57.937
From that time to
this, Phil Morrison

00:17:57.937 --> 00:18:02.927
has played a central role in
development and monotonically

00:18:02.927 --> 00:18:06.919
increasing respectability
of the subject.

00:18:06.919 --> 00:18:11.909
Right now, there is a search
program going on at Harvard,

00:18:11.909 --> 00:18:16.400
Massachusetts, led by
Paul Horowitz of Harvard,

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:22.887
in which 8.4 million separate
channels, each much narrower

00:18:22.887 --> 00:18:26.879
than a hertz, are being
examined simultaneously

00:18:26.879 --> 00:18:31.370
as the telescope scans
the skies, as visible

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:33.865
from Harvard in Massachusetts.

00:18:33.865 --> 00:18:38.855
And a still much more
sophisticated program

00:18:38.855 --> 00:18:45.715
is being worked on by the
National Aeronautics and Space

00:18:45.715 --> 00:18:46.340
Administration.

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:49.833
In both of these efforts,
Phil has played a central role

00:18:49.833 --> 00:18:54.823
sharing the NASA committees, and
providing inspiration, insight,

00:18:54.823 --> 00:18:57.318
and motivation.

00:18:57.318 --> 00:19:05.302
We, of course, don't know how
these searches will turn up.

00:19:05.302 --> 00:19:10.292
There are many aspects
of it that are strange,

00:19:10.292 --> 00:19:13.785
and many of the
descriptions of the subject

00:19:13.785 --> 00:19:17.777
have metaphors and
insight which go straight

00:19:17.777 --> 00:19:19.274
back to Phil Morrison.

00:19:19.274 --> 00:19:22.183
Many of those who
speak them may not even

00:19:22.183 --> 00:19:23.266
know where they came from.

00:19:23.266 --> 00:19:27.757
For example, the now
very common comment

00:19:27.757 --> 00:19:30.751
that we hear, that
I myself have used,

00:19:30.751 --> 00:19:35.741
that we must expect
monologue, not dialogue,

00:19:35.741 --> 00:19:38.735
because of the great
distances, but that

00:19:38.735 --> 00:19:41.230
is not as big a deficit
as you might imagine,

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:46.220
that Socrates speaks to us even
if we do not speak to Socrates.

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:50.711
We treasure certain kinds
of one-way communication.

00:19:50.711 --> 00:19:56.200
That insight and metaphor
goes straight back to Phil.

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:00.192
How this search will wind
up, no one, of course, knows.

00:20:00.192 --> 00:20:04.683
It may turn out that there will
be a comprehensive, very narrow

00:20:04.683 --> 00:20:08.675
band search of both the
northern and southern skies

00:20:08.675 --> 00:20:10.950
for a period of decades.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:13.680
If on the other hand,
we would receive

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:20.010
a signal of intelligent origin,
even before it was decrypted,

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:24.090
we would have learned
something of the very greatest

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:25.250
importance--

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.301
that there are other
beings out there,

00:20:27.301 --> 00:20:29.550
that they are almost certainly
more advanced than we--

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:33.150
because the statistical chance
of them being within this very

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.360
brief few decade long period
after the invention of radio

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.540
astronomy has very
low probability,

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:43.560
and therefore that they have
avoided self-destruction.

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:47.820
The fact that that is possible
is knowledge well worth having.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:52.830
And of course, a successful
detection and decryption would,

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:58.230
in ways that we can imagine
but cannot accurately foresee,

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:01.410
change the history
of the human species.

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:04.620
If that were ever to
happen, there is no question

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:09.510
that we would all owe the
greatest debt of gratitude Phil

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:11.880
Morrison.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.290
I don't want you to
think that this openness

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:21.320
to speculative
ideas is untempered.

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:23.340
In Phil Morrison's
personality, there

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:31.080
is also a very appropriate dose
of scientific skepticism, which

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:33.810
you can see in many
different areas.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:37.290
His famous remark, I
will believe a black hole

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:39.390
when I see one, is an example.

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:51.260
And it is exactly the
tension between this openness

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:52.880
and this skepticism
which I think

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:58.460
is one of Phil's great
virtues, one well

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:01.460
worth emulating if we can.

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:07.850
So let's think of Phil as
pioneering this subject

00:22:07.850 --> 00:22:10.400
of the search for
extraterrestrial intelligence

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:15.530
at the same time, in
the '60s, that there

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:21.800
was a kind of worldwide epidemic
of people who believed we were,

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:28.620
at that same moment, being
visited by what were called

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:31.910
unidentified flying objects.

00:22:31.910 --> 00:22:35.450
This had captivated
many, and not

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:40.640
just the purchases
of weekly newspapers

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:44.330
sold in supermarkets.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:49.040
I can remember James McDonald,
a respected atmospheric

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:52.040
physicist, a member of the
National Academy of Sciences,

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:56.960
who believed that there were
some anecdotal cases which

00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:59.000
were so compelling
as to make us really

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:02.750
reconsider the possibility
that we were being visited.

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:09.800
And I arranged for McDonald to
present to Phil his best cases.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:16.030
And somehow, while being
extremely kind and generous,

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:20.970
the balloon was punctured
in a period of about 15,

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:27.680
20 minutes of extremely
friendly conversation.

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:34.010
And Phil there did, I think for
many of us, a great service.

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:38.750
At the same time, Phil
was replete with anecdotes

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:42.050
of an enlightening
nature, one of which

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:45.230
I will retell, but
from memory, and so

00:23:45.230 --> 00:23:49.100
if I get some details wrong,
please forgive me, Phil.

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:51.380
The story goes like this--

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:57.470
a New Mexico highway patrolman
is driving along a road

00:23:57.470 --> 00:24:01.490
that he had many times
before driven along.

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:04.520
And suddenly before
his eyes, he sees

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:11.120
a great metallic saucer-shaped
object land in somebody's farm.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:13.610
And it's right
there on the ground.

00:24:13.610 --> 00:24:15.290
He sees it.

00:24:15.290 --> 00:24:22.010
He happens to know some
scientists at Los Alamos.

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:25.810
He calls them up, explains
what happened right there

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:32.540
at the highway, and they
say, keep your eye on it

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:35.360
and we'll be there
as soon as we can.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.890
So let's imagine the
sun rising in the sky,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:43.760
the highway patrolman sitting
there in his patrol car,

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:49.430
and the saucer-shaped object
sitting there in the field.

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:52.490
The scientists arrive.

00:24:52.490 --> 00:24:59.070
They and the highway patrolman
then approach this object.

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:03.260
The farmer is there
on the farm, and he

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:07.700
seems totally oblivious
of this strange object.

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:10.530
Could it be that it was
invisible to the farmer

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:15.350
but visible to the scientists
and the highway patrolman?

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:19.550
They approached
the farmer and say,

00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:23.180
could you tell us a little
about that UFO that just landed?

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:26.360
The farmer turns around, looks
straight at it, and says,

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:26.975
what UFO?

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:31.880
Well, they talk a little
bit, and it turns out

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:35.510
that what had been
thought was a UFO was just

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:39.890
a kind of lenticular metallic
storage structure that

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:41.750
had been there for years.

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:47.810
And there was only one problem
in the highway patrolman's

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:48.710
argument.

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:54.307
He mistook his glimpse to
mean the thing had landed

00:25:54.307 --> 00:25:55.640
and he had never seen it before.

00:25:55.640 --> 00:26:01.250
Phil's lesson was that in a
scheme of inferential argument,

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:05.480
you need only break one link
in the scheme for the argument

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.490
to collapse.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:11.690
I also remember-- it was a
time when something called

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:14.780
the Bermuda Triangle
was imagined to be

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:17.037
eating ships and airplanes.

00:26:20.240 --> 00:26:25.970
And Phil raised the question of,
why aren't trains being eaten?

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:34.530
Ships in the ocean, airplanes
flying over the ocean,

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:37.620
there is a first
order hypothesis

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:41.760
about what happens to make
them disappear-- they sink.

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:45.950
Trains, on the other hand,
do not have that option,

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:47.910
and therefore here we
have the possibility

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:52.260
of normalizing the hypothesis.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:56.419
And then, as soon as
that point sank in,

00:26:56.419 --> 00:26:58.710
Phil then would come up with
a story about a train that

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:00.630
did disappear.

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:02.310
Well, maybe it wasn't
exactly a train.

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:04.980
The story, as I remember
it, goes like this--

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:11.910
a large rotor for
some rotary machinery

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:14.490
was being shipped by
train on a railroad

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:21.660
flat car in a vertical position
with guy wires supporting it.

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:24.220
It left the manufacturing plant.

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:27.525
The train arrived at whatever
the transhipment depot where it

00:27:27.525 --> 00:27:29.250
was supposed to be taken off.

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:32.440
And the flat car was
there, but no rotor.

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:35.730
It was much too large
to have been hijacked.

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.550
And they went over
every mile of the track.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:40.830
It had mysteriously disappeared.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:45.750
And this problem was
unsolved for a long period

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:48.420
of time, decades,
until in draining

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:51.180
a swamp a few miles
from the railroad track

00:27:51.180 --> 00:27:52.230
they found the rotor.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:55.620
It had obviously
broken its guy wires.

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:58.500
And I don't know how
tall the thing was,

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:00.870
10 meters or
something, had rolled

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:05.730
a few miles down to the swamp.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:08.700
Can you imagine what it must
have been like if anyone

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:11.100
had simply been wandering by?

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.920
One of Phil's most
remarkable characteristics

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:29.550
is his ability as a teacher.

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.131
I come from Cornell University.

00:28:31.131 --> 00:28:33.630
It has been a long time since
Phil was on the faculty there,

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:37.620
but his influence
is still palpable.

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:41.310
People who attend his
lectures never forget them.

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:45.210
He has had a powerful
influence on a course curricula

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:46.830
for children.

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:51.815
In films and television
programs, I'm sure many of you

00:28:51.815 --> 00:28:54.900
have seen the
remarkable film that he

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:58.140
and Phylis Morrison
and the Eames' did

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:01.770
called Powers of Ten.

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:11.310
Philip Daly, who is responsible
for the remarkable set

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:16.790
of science films in the BBC
over the last few decades,

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.500
stopped by just before
I came up to ask

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:26.190
me to mention that Phil has been
a major source of inspiration

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:30.630
for the BBC in even attempting
to do science films.

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:35.880
And I can certainly say
that for the project

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:39.870
I've been involved with in
television science education,

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:43.750
Phil has also been a leading
source of inspiration.

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:46.770
His articles in Scientific
American, book reviews

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:50.820
were mentioned by Francis Low.

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:53.640
I think it's fair to say
that many people learn more

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:56.190
from the reviews than from
actually reading the books.

00:29:59.670 --> 00:30:06.740
There is a kind of condensation
down to the essence

00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:10.730
of the thing, which is hard to
extract one's self in reading

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:11.940
the book.

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.450
And I think these
reviews are also

00:30:14.450 --> 00:30:18.200
marked by a generosity of
spirit, in which insights

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:22.420
are attributed to the authors
which they often never had.

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:23.673
It's just Phil who had them.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:33.890
Phil, in teaching
science to the public,

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:36.800
constantly stresses
not only what we know,

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:40.640
but how it is we
came to know it.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:45.290
He constantly stresses respect
for the work of the hands

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:50.330
as well as the mind, following
that ancient tradition

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:53.300
of the Ionian scientists,
who in certain sense

00:30:53.300 --> 00:30:56.150
can be said to have founded
the scientific tradition

00:30:56.150 --> 00:30:58.100
of the west.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:31:01.190
Phil exemplifies
the responsibility

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:05.720
that I personally believe
scientists have to communicate

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:06.890
science to the public.

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:12.530
There are many reasons
why it's important.

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:16.050
One is just that
we love our subject

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:18.420
and are enthusiastic about it.

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:20.810
It's very natural to
try to communicate

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:24.500
what you love to others.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:29.000
It's one of those
non-zero-sum games,

00:31:29.000 --> 00:31:33.230
like love itself, in which
every time you give, you gain.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:39.530
In addition, there
are clear sort

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:41.600
of naked scientific
self-interest reasons.

00:31:41.600 --> 00:31:44.720
If we wish the public
coffers to support science,

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:48.140
we have a clear
responsibility to explain what

00:31:48.140 --> 00:31:51.610
the virtues of science are.

00:31:51.610 --> 00:31:53.860
And I think we are a
scientific species.

00:31:53.860 --> 00:31:58.760
It's one of the very few
things we do uniquely

00:31:58.760 --> 00:32:02.430
that other animals do not do.

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:06.770
I think every person on the
planet is, on some level,

00:32:06.770 --> 00:32:08.030
a scientist.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:09.890
But unfortunately,
school curricula

00:32:09.890 --> 00:32:13.910
and the social
conventions work not

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:18.510
to encourage, but to discourage
this sort of interest.

00:32:18.510 --> 00:32:21.110
And as scientists, I think
we have a responsibility

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:22.670
to counter that.

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:24.980
And then there is the clear
social responsibility,

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:29.690
that we live in a time,
civilization, culture,

00:32:29.690 --> 00:32:34.850
deeply involved with, dependent
upon, science and technology.

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:38.630
And the clearest prescription
for disaster is for the public

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:40.820
not to know about
science and technology.

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:44.000
How can the public
manage their own future

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:46.040
if they don't know some
of the essential tools

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:48.320
for doing that management?

00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:52.520
So I don't know what
mix of these reasons

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:57.650
has involved Philip Morrison
in science education

00:32:57.650 --> 00:33:02.570
for the broad public,
but there are many of us

00:33:02.570 --> 00:33:04.040
grateful that he has done so.

00:33:06.700 --> 00:33:13.750
On the issue of the mix of
openness and skepticism,

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:22.230
I want also to mention Phil's
courageous and very long

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:26.020
standing opposition
to the continuation

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:30.390
of the mad nuclear arms race.

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:33.190
Phil was involved in
the Manhattan Project.

00:33:33.190 --> 00:33:35.630
He armed the Nagasaki bomb.

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:39.730
He was as deeply
involved as anyone

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:41.770
in the development
of nuclear weapons

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:45.730
to bring an end to
World War II and to beat

00:33:45.730 --> 00:33:51.640
the Nazis to this weapon
of great destructive power.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:55.090
On the other hand, soon
after the end of the war,

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:59.470
it became clear to many
scientists, including Phil,

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:03.580
that there were grave dangers
of continuing in this direction.

00:34:03.580 --> 00:34:08.260
And he has spoken out often
and courageously on this issue.

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:13.000
I can remember being at
a dinner at the Pentagon

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:19.989
in a previous administration in
which a secretary of defense,

00:34:19.989 --> 00:34:26.290
much more moderate than
the one now in office,

00:34:26.290 --> 00:34:31.840
was vehemently decrying a study
that Phil and a group of people

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:35.980
with him called the Boston
Study Group had put together

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:39.520
on why it was in the
national interest

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:42.701
to make massive cuts in the
Department of Defense budget,

00:34:42.701 --> 00:34:44.575
the contention being
that it would strengthen

00:34:44.575 --> 00:34:46.480
the national security
of the United States

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:49.360
if you did it in
a prescribed way.

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:55.120
As you might imagine, that
did not fall on receptive ears

00:34:55.120 --> 00:34:58.090
at the Department of Defense.

00:34:58.090 --> 00:35:00.400
It was ahead of its time.

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:04.420
I think that time is coming.

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:12.920
There are many issues
related to the cessation,

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:15.370
or at least the slowing down
of the nuclear arms race.

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:19.450
I just want to mention
one, and that's

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:24.850
the issue of continued
testing of nuclear weapons.

00:35:24.850 --> 00:35:29.680
In the 1963 Limited
Test Ban Treaty,

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:33.760
there is in the
preamble a moving phrase

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:36.390
about how the United
States and the Soviet Union

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:41.440
pledge their efforts
to ending the arms race

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:46.370
and moving towards a ban on the
testing of all nuclear weapons.

00:35:46.370 --> 00:35:48.560
That was 1963.

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:51.070
In the 23 years
since then, there

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:55.150
has not been much progress.

00:35:55.150 --> 00:36:00.590
In 1963, there was a remarkable
moment in recent history.

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:04.300
The United States and the Soviet
Union with the Cuban Missile

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:09.460
Crisis had managed to scare
each other almost to death.

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:12.850
And for a very good
reason, Bobby Kennedy

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:16.840
wrote that he thought
that there was a 50/50

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:21.550
chance of a central nuclear
exchange between the United

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:24.310
States and the Soviet
Union over that incident.

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:26.800
For a moment, the leaders
of the two countries

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:31.990
had focused their attention
on the nuclear arms race.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:34.600
And in a speech at
American University,

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:37.570
the American President,
John F. Kennedy,

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:40.390
announced a unilateral
US moratorium

00:36:40.390 --> 00:36:44.350
on the further testing
of nuclear weapons.

00:36:44.350 --> 00:36:46.270
The next day, the full
text of that speech

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:48.280
was published,
remarkably enough,

00:36:48.280 --> 00:36:51.490
in Izvestia and in Pravda.

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:56.140
Within the week, the Soviet
Premier, Nikita Khrushchev,

00:36:56.140 --> 00:36:58.450
announced a unilateral
Soviet moratorium

00:36:58.450 --> 00:37:04.150
on further above-ground testing
of nuclear weapons, which

00:37:04.150 --> 00:37:06.520
was merely
coincidentally happening

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:09.370
in the same week as the
American moratorium.

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:15.400
But what we had
was a de facto ban

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:17.650
on further above-ground
nuclear testing

00:37:17.650 --> 00:37:21.550
by the two principal
nuclear powers.

00:37:21.550 --> 00:37:24.610
What then happened is
a remarkable moment

00:37:24.610 --> 00:37:27.370
in recent history, in which
the two countries vied

00:37:27.370 --> 00:37:32.030
with each other to be nice.

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:35.350
The Soviets removed
their objection

00:37:35.350 --> 00:37:39.310
to the stationing of
neutral observers in Yemen.

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:41.290
The Americans removed
their objections

00:37:41.290 --> 00:37:45.670
to the credentials of the
proposed Hungarian delegation

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:47.750
to the United Nations.

00:37:47.750 --> 00:37:52.030
There was a mutual
doffing of the hats.

00:37:52.030 --> 00:37:56.350
Before things got
out of hand, however,

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:59.050
an American academic
named Barghoorn

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:03.610
was arrested in Moscow
on grounds of espionage.

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:06.610
And the climate heated up.

00:38:06.610 --> 00:38:14.440
And there was a real danger of
the test ban treaty unraveling.

00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:21.970
But Mr. Kennedy made
private remonstrances

00:38:21.970 --> 00:38:23.710
with Mr. Khrushchev.

00:38:23.710 --> 00:38:27.940
And Khrushchev then released
the American academic,

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:33.250
saying that while he,
Khrushchev, was not convinced

00:38:33.250 --> 00:38:36.040
that he was innocent
of espionage charges,

00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:39.700
because the American president
wished it so strongly,

00:38:39.700 --> 00:38:46.510
he, Khrushchev, as a gesture
of humanitarian dedication,

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:47.650
released him.

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:55.030
And then it was time to
convert the de facto treaty

00:38:55.030 --> 00:38:57.130
to de jure treaty.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:58.870
Averell Harriman
was sent to Moscow

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:02.260
as an ambassador,
got off the airplane.

00:39:02.260 --> 00:39:05.740
A reporter from TASS said to him
something like, Mr. ambassador,

00:39:05.740 --> 00:39:08.980
considering the grave tensions
between the two countries

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:12.940
and the difficulties of
such a treaty, how long do

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:15.880
you think it will be before
a successful treaty can

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:17.110
be negotiated?

00:39:17.110 --> 00:39:20.830
Harriman said, if Mr.
Khrushchev wishes such a treaty

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:22.990
half as much as
Mr. Kennedy, I'll

00:39:22.990 --> 00:39:25.180
be back on this
airplane in two weeks.

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:29.260
13 days later, the 1963
Test Ban Treaty was signed.

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:32.530
And from that day to this,
no American or Soviet

00:39:32.530 --> 00:39:35.590
nuclear weapon has been tested
on the ground, in the air,

00:39:35.590 --> 00:39:38.050
or in space.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:43.090
On August 6 of 1985, on the 40th
anniversary of the Hiroshima

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:48.460
bomb, the Soviet Union announced
a unilateral moratorium

00:39:48.460 --> 00:39:52.720
on further underground testing.

00:39:52.720 --> 00:39:55.780
The American response
has been to explode

00:39:55.780 --> 00:40:01.840
20 underground nuclear
weapons and to contemptuously

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:05.980
reject the Soviet offer.

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:10.360
This is an opportunity
which may not recur.

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:15.130
It is very clear that the
longer the Soviets continue

00:40:15.130 --> 00:40:17.394
their moratorium,
the greater will

00:40:17.394 --> 00:40:19.060
be the pressure on
the Soviet leadership

00:40:19.060 --> 00:40:22.690
from the nuclear arms
establishment in the Soviet

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:27.730
Union, and it may be that the
two nations will perpetually

00:40:27.730 --> 00:40:31.450
remain out of phase.

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:34.300
What seems to me
is our objective

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:39.630
is to arrange things so that
they are in phase long enough

00:40:39.630 --> 00:40:45.520
in reasonableness to make
a significant decline

00:40:45.520 --> 00:40:51.100
in this mad race to oblivion.

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:53.500
An American private group,
the Natural Resources Defense

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:57.790
Council, has established a
seismic monitoring station

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:00.790
in the Soviet test area
near Semipalatinsk.

00:41:00.790 --> 00:41:08.140
Of So now that argument
has been swept away,

00:41:08.140 --> 00:41:10.090
and we hear other
sorts of arguments.

00:41:10.090 --> 00:41:12.010
To give an idea
of the desperation

00:41:12.010 --> 00:41:14.474
of the justification,
here are three ways

00:41:14.474 --> 00:41:16.390
that have been proposed
by this administration

00:41:16.390 --> 00:41:18.120
that the Soviets can cheat.

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:24.960
One, they can wait for a natural
earthquake in Central Asia

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:28.060
and test their weapons then.

00:41:28.060 --> 00:41:29.980
Even there, by the way,
the seismic signatures

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:34.160
of natural earthquakes and
nuclear weapons are different.

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:36.200
Two, they could decouple.

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:38.320
They could make a vast
underground cavity

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:42.780
and muffle the sound of
the nuclear explosion.

00:41:42.780 --> 00:41:46.120
Yeah, I guess sound
is the right word.

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:48.650
But for any weapon
of significant yield,

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:53.280
you have to excavate such
an enormous volume of earth

00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:57.820
that it should be readily
detectable by satellite

00:41:57.820 --> 00:41:58.990
reconnaissance.

00:41:58.990 --> 00:42:02.950
And then the pride of
place in these arguments

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:06.220
is the third argument due
to Edward Teller, which

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:10.000
is that the Soviets will cheat
by exploding nuclear weapons

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:11.380
on the far side of the sun.

00:42:25.660 --> 00:42:31.120
Stopping testing
is a long-lever arm

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:34.780
to ceasing the nuclear
arms race and to bring us

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:38.710
into a regime in
which we can set

00:42:38.710 --> 00:42:42.100
about the urgent task of
making major, verifiable,

00:42:42.100 --> 00:42:48.430
and bilateral cuts in this
absurd joint arsenal of almost

00:42:48.430 --> 00:42:53.850
60,000 weapons of
mass destruction.

00:42:53.850 --> 00:42:58.810
And if we succeed in doing that,
some measure of inspiration

00:42:58.810 --> 00:43:00.850
will have come again
from Philip Morrison.

00:43:04.040 --> 00:43:08.500
I want to mention just one
more topic before I sum up,

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:12.350
and that has to do
with exploration.

00:43:12.350 --> 00:43:19.070
Phil is an explorer, an
explorer in many senses,

00:43:19.070 --> 00:43:24.380
but also an explorer in the
traditional sense of going out

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:28.130
to new lands.

00:43:28.130 --> 00:43:30.320
He has traveled widely.

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:33.230
But the kind of exploration
I'm talking about

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:39.840
is real, old time
exploration with explorers.

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:45.730
Phil is on the advisory board
of the Planetary Society,

00:43:45.730 --> 00:43:50.160
a 100,000 member worldwide
organization that

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:53.640
is devoted to exploration
of the solar system,

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:56.580
as well as the search for
extraterrestrial intelligence.

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:01.105
And I'd just like to give you
a vision of something that

00:44:01.105 --> 00:44:01.605
is possible.

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:11.940
It is a joint US-Soviet, manned
and womanned mission to Mars.

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:19.700
There's not any appropriate
word to say manned and womanned.

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:22.050
Manned certainly seems
wrong, because there

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:23.205
are woman astronauts.

00:44:23.205 --> 00:44:25.290
It leaves out half the species.

00:44:25.290 --> 00:44:34.030
And some of the alternatives,
like crewed missions,

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:36.109
lends itself to
misunderstanding.

00:44:42.530 --> 00:44:44.000
Mars is a world of wonders.

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:47.420
The scientific justification
for going there is very clear.

00:44:47.420 --> 00:44:50.580
For one thing, a billion years
ago there was liquid water

00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:51.920
all over the planet.

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:53.022
Rivers were gushing.

00:44:53.022 --> 00:44:53.730
There were lakes.

00:44:53.730 --> 00:44:56.210
There's even a contention
that there were oceans on Mars

00:44:56.210 --> 00:44:57.380
a billion years ago.

00:44:57.380 --> 00:44:59.540
Something very important
happened between then

00:44:59.540 --> 00:45:02.850
and now in terms of
massive climatic change,

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:05.480
and it might even be of
practical significance

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:09.560
for us living on a
nearby planet not all

00:45:09.560 --> 00:45:11.800
that different from Mars to
understand what happened.

00:45:14.900 --> 00:45:20.270
I won't go into the scientific
reasons for going to Mars,

00:45:20.270 --> 00:45:23.210
but they are manifold.

00:45:23.210 --> 00:45:25.130
But to send a mission
with human crews

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:28.310
requires, it seems to me, more
than scientific justification,

00:45:28.310 --> 00:45:31.670
because people are much more
expensive, between 10 and 100

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:35.240
times more expensive, than some
comparable robotic mission,

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:38.450
which could do
very well in terms

00:45:38.450 --> 00:45:41.930
of science and exploration.

00:45:41.930 --> 00:45:46.130
And the only reason to
spend that sort of money--

00:45:46.130 --> 00:45:48.170
by the way, it's not a
huge amount of money.

00:45:48.170 --> 00:45:51.270
It's a tiny fraction
of Star Wars.

00:45:51.270 --> 00:45:53.750
It's in fact in constant
dollars less than the cost

00:45:53.750 --> 00:45:54.770
of the Apollo program.

00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:56.870
It's about the cost of a
major strategic weapons

00:45:56.870 --> 00:46:02.060
system, which we seem to have
an endless supply of money

00:46:02.060 --> 00:46:03.560
to support.

00:46:03.560 --> 00:46:06.770
But imagine such a mission.

00:46:06.770 --> 00:46:09.020
What could its
political purpose be?

00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:11.570
It can't be competition
with the Soviets in space.

00:46:11.570 --> 00:46:19.280
That is an issue, despite
the president's deep problems

00:46:19.280 --> 00:46:22.410
with the American space program.

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:24.560
We have had enough
successes in space

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:26.721
that we don't have to do that.

00:46:26.721 --> 00:46:28.970
But the idea of the United
States and the Soviet Union

00:46:28.970 --> 00:46:33.620
together, doing an exploration
on behalf of the human species,

00:46:33.620 --> 00:46:36.620
paving a way into
the 21st century,

00:46:36.620 --> 00:46:40.040
capturing the imagination of
people all over the planet--

00:46:40.040 --> 00:46:42.720
that seems to be
something worthwhile.

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:45.440
A mission which
would for some years

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:49.160
be before the public
eye, in the construction

00:46:49.160 --> 00:46:52.330
of the interplanetary transfer
vehicle in Earth's orbit,

00:46:52.330 --> 00:46:56.630
in the voyage to Mars and the
setting down on the Martian

00:46:56.630 --> 00:47:00.080
surface, Americans
and Soviets together

00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:05.660
in the exploration of Mars, some
wheeled vehicle, for example,

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:09.440
down an ancient
Martian river valley,

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:11.860
and the return to Earth--

00:47:11.860 --> 00:47:17.780
at every step citizens of these
two countries working together.

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:19.820
I think it could do wonders.

00:47:19.820 --> 00:47:24.260
And it uses, notice, exactly
the same sorts of rocket

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:27.830
and nuclear and computer
technology that is also

00:47:27.830 --> 00:47:29.780
used to drive the arms race.

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:36.050
I think there is a way to
use the same technology that

00:47:36.050 --> 00:47:39.860
has created some of the
very serious problems

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:41.390
that we have here
on Earth, a place

00:47:41.390 --> 00:47:44.540
contaminated with
60,000 nuclear weapons,

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:49.760
to undo it by going elsewhere.

00:47:49.760 --> 00:47:54.530
Well, I am grateful
for the opportunity

00:47:54.530 --> 00:48:01.100
to be able to speak
to you in honor

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:06.060
of Philip Morrison,
physicist, astronomer,

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:12.860
SETI pioneer, peerless teacher,
weaponeer when it counted,

00:48:12.860 --> 00:48:20.300
peace activist when it counted,
pasta manufacturer, explorer,

00:48:20.300 --> 00:48:25.400
master of metaphor, inspiration
to generations of scientists

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:27.500
and others.

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:30.320
I should say that
in much of this,

00:48:30.320 --> 00:48:35.300
we can see the wisdom and
vitality and tenderness

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:38.630
of Phylis Morrison,
who has clearly

00:48:38.630 --> 00:48:45.590
played an important part in
Phil's manifold activities.

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:49.970
Phil has helped students,
colleagues, and many readers

00:48:49.970 --> 00:48:54.740
join the universe,
but he has also

00:48:54.740 --> 00:48:57.260
helped a much vaster
audience of people

00:48:57.260 --> 00:49:00.140
all over the world,
many of whom may never

00:49:00.140 --> 00:49:03.560
have even heard his name.

00:49:03.560 --> 00:49:09.170
His kindness, his generosity
of spirit, his insights,

00:49:09.170 --> 00:49:13.100
his enthusiasm,
his social concern

00:49:13.100 --> 00:49:16.310
have made him a model
for other scientists

00:49:16.310 --> 00:49:21.598
in the rare art of being a
fully developed human being.

00:49:21.598 --> 00:49:22.594
Thank you.

00:49:27.902 --> 00:49:29.068
FRANCIS LOW: Are there que--

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:50.970
I think we have
time for questions,

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:54.565
if people would like to ask any.

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:02.895
Yes sir, yes.

00:50:02.895 --> 00:50:08.970
AUDIENCE: I've heard
a recent speech

00:50:08.970 --> 00:50:12.365
that you gave to a group of
journalists on public radio.

00:50:12.365 --> 00:50:17.215
Would you care to comment on
the state of science journalism

00:50:17.215 --> 00:50:18.185
right now?

00:50:21.045 --> 00:50:22.420
CARL SAGAN: Yeah,
this was a talk

00:50:22.420 --> 00:50:26.440
I gave a couple of days ago
at the National Press Club,

00:50:26.440 --> 00:50:29.980
not on that issue,
but I was asked that.

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:33.820
Well, it's mixed.

00:50:33.820 --> 00:50:38.290
And I guess the easiest is,
virtually every newspaper

00:50:38.290 --> 00:50:42.460
in America has a daily
astrology column.

00:50:42.460 --> 00:50:45.520
How many of them have even
a weekly astronomy column?

00:50:45.520 --> 00:50:49.090
That's certainly
one measure of it.

00:50:49.090 --> 00:50:55.900
Another measure is when
the Nobel Prizes in science

00:50:55.900 --> 00:51:00.370
are announced every year, when's
the last time you can recall

00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:04.600
getting a coherent explanation
on radio or television

00:51:04.600 --> 00:51:08.410
of what they won
their prizes for?

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:11.395
It's almost entirely
oh, yeah, I woke up

00:51:11.395 --> 00:51:12.520
in the middle of the night.

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:13.210
I was surprised.

00:51:13.210 --> 00:51:15.084
I didn't believe the
reporter from Stockholm.

00:51:15.084 --> 00:51:15.870
My wife was happy.

00:51:20.650 --> 00:51:22.540
When do we find
out what these guys

00:51:22.540 --> 00:51:25.000
did to win their Nobel Prizes?

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:27.460
I think that that's
another indication

00:51:27.460 --> 00:51:29.440
of the state of journalism.

00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:31.480
Another is, you
look at people who

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:33.850
are called science
reporters for the networks,

00:51:33.850 --> 00:51:37.030
and you find that they
don't report science.

00:51:37.030 --> 00:51:42.100
It's not scientific ideas,
theories, discoveries.

00:51:42.100 --> 00:51:44.324
It's almost entirely
medicine and technology.

00:51:44.324 --> 00:51:45.865
There ought to be
a medical reporter.

00:51:45.865 --> 00:51:47.600
There ought to be a
technology reporter.

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:49.308
But there ought to be
a science reporter.

00:51:51.550 --> 00:51:54.220
I think the general
level of teaching science

00:51:54.220 --> 00:51:59.470
in the public schools
is woefully low.

00:51:59.470 --> 00:52:02.170
I think scientific
attitudes of thought,

00:52:02.170 --> 00:52:05.890
including skepticism
of received wisdom,

00:52:05.890 --> 00:52:11.290
is desperately needed in various
social, economical, political,

00:52:11.290 --> 00:52:15.400
and even religious aspects of
our society, and isn't there.

00:52:15.400 --> 00:52:17.890
And this may in fact
explain part of the reason

00:52:17.890 --> 00:52:19.390
that scientific
attitudes of thought

00:52:19.390 --> 00:52:22.930
are not taught by and
large in the schools.

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:25.540
I think this is simply suicidal.

00:52:25.540 --> 00:52:29.530
I think science ought to be
talked about on some level

00:52:29.530 --> 00:52:32.080
over dinner tables.

00:52:32.080 --> 00:52:33.700
Why is it all right
for newspapers

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:41.500
to have many, many pages of
fine print on the stock market?

00:52:41.500 --> 00:52:45.980
Really very arcane, lots of
abbreviations, lots of numbers,

00:52:45.980 --> 00:52:47.055
fractions.

00:52:51.420 --> 00:52:55.460
Why is it all right to have
box scores of baseball games,

00:52:55.460 --> 00:53:04.710
you know, with
abbreviations, HB, RBI.

00:53:04.710 --> 00:53:08.117
No explanation, you're
supposed to understand that.

00:53:08.117 --> 00:53:10.200
You pick up your daily
paper and you figure, well,

00:53:10.200 --> 00:53:12.840
just being a person
in this society,

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:15.810
I'm expected to know something
about stocks and bonds.

00:53:15.810 --> 00:53:19.867
I'm expected to know something
about baseball and football.

00:53:19.867 --> 00:53:22.200
I guess I'm not expected to
know anything about science.

00:53:22.200 --> 00:53:23.850
There's not any of it in here.

00:53:23.850 --> 00:53:26.910
I maintain that there's an
enormous amount of science that

00:53:26.910 --> 00:53:30.470
can be taught in
newspapers and elsewhere

00:53:30.470 --> 00:53:33.160
that's a whole lot easier than
the stock page and the sports

00:53:33.160 --> 00:53:33.660
pages.

00:53:37.290 --> 00:53:38.396
Yes, please?

00:53:38.396 --> 00:53:41.710
AUDIENCE: What's the present
status of nuclear winter?

00:53:41.710 --> 00:53:43.160
CARL SAGAN: Present status of?

00:53:43.160 --> 00:53:44.202
AUDIENCE: Nuclear winter.

00:53:44.202 --> 00:53:46.076
CARL SAGAN: Present
status of nuclear winter.

00:53:46.076 --> 00:53:48.328
Well, I'm glad to say
it's not here yet.

00:53:55.160 --> 00:54:01.220
Nuclear winter is the
contention that if we

00:54:01.220 --> 00:54:04.550
are so foolish as to permit
a nuclear war to happen,

00:54:04.550 --> 00:54:08.120
the fine particles of soot
and dust that would be raised

00:54:08.120 --> 00:54:11.690
would spread over
large land areas,

00:54:11.690 --> 00:54:15.560
would darken and
cool the earth enough

00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:21.770
to produce a significant
additional peril

00:54:21.770 --> 00:54:25.930
for those who survive the
consequences of nuclear war.

00:54:25.930 --> 00:54:27.530
And while there are
continuing debates

00:54:27.530 --> 00:54:31.340
on just how much the
temperature decline would be

00:54:31.340 --> 00:54:33.530
and what the
duration would be, I

00:54:33.530 --> 00:54:39.260
think it is fair to say that the
consensus of scientific opinion

00:54:39.260 --> 00:54:41.912
is that it would
be very serious.

00:54:41.912 --> 00:54:44.120
Or maybe the best I can do
is to quote the Department

00:54:44.120 --> 00:54:47.300
of Defense, which proposes
that the consequences

00:54:47.300 --> 00:54:51.650
of nuclear winter
would kill perhaps

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:56.240
as many people as the direct
consequences, which have been

00:54:56.240 --> 00:54:58.280
variously estimated
for a central exchange

00:54:58.280 --> 00:55:02.790
as between a few hundred
million and two billion people.

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:03.922
Other questions?

00:55:03.922 --> 00:55:05.280
Yes, please?

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:07.272
AUDIENCE: You talked
about various doctrines

00:55:07.272 --> 00:55:14.212
throughout history,
given the idea that there

00:55:14.212 --> 00:55:16.188
is something [INAUDIBLE].

00:55:16.188 --> 00:55:20.150
Suppose [INAUDIBLE]
two people are

00:55:20.150 --> 00:55:23.004
arguing about [INAUDIBLE]
the probability of that

00:55:23.004 --> 00:55:23.927
is very small.

00:55:23.927 --> 00:55:24.510
Suppose that--

00:55:24.510 --> 00:55:26.634
CARL SAGAN: I'm sorry, I
missed that last sentence.

00:55:26.634 --> 00:55:31.690
AUDIENCE: The probability of
two people arguing and being

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:34.035
[INAUDIBLE],, like you
just said, is very small.

00:55:34.035 --> 00:55:38.725
Suppose that right now,
the two superpowers

00:55:38.725 --> 00:55:44.681
started earnestly trying
to have a unified view

00:55:44.681 --> 00:55:48.529
on the human creature.

00:55:48.529 --> 00:55:50.576
And how soon do
you think that we

00:55:50.576 --> 00:55:54.050
could have a unified
world, a planet which

00:55:54.050 --> 00:55:59.490
acts as one entity,
looks towards the space.

00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:01.664
CARL SAGAN: How soon?

00:56:01.664 --> 00:56:04.080
If a miracle happened and the
United States and the Soviet

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:07.350
Union were to agree on what
to do, how soon before we

00:56:07.350 --> 00:56:09.810
could have a unified world?

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:13.740
I have a perfect quote
from Philip Morrison.

00:56:13.740 --> 00:56:15.510
Prophecy is a lost art.

00:56:23.446 --> 00:56:25.320
Francis Low says especially
about the future.

00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:38.580
Nevertheless, I'd like to stress
that the problems that face us

00:56:38.580 --> 00:56:40.130
were made by people.

00:56:40.130 --> 00:56:45.540
There is nothing about them
that cannot be unmade by people.

00:56:45.540 --> 00:56:49.620
We have made
extraordinary changes

00:56:49.620 --> 00:56:53.640
in what we consider obvious.

00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:56.730
And of course, when we make
those changes, then we forget.

00:56:56.730 --> 00:56:59.220
But for example,
there was a time not

00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:03.150
so long ago when the view
of the divine right of kings

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:05.820
was widely held worldwide.

00:57:05.820 --> 00:57:10.530
Famous scholars wrote famous
textbooks on this subject.

00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:14.760
There was a vast, vested
political interest

00:57:14.760 --> 00:57:21.880
to maintain this view,
especially among kings.

00:57:21.880 --> 00:57:26.480
And yet-- in fact,
there was even a war

00:57:26.480 --> 00:57:29.950
fought here partly
on that issue.

00:57:29.950 --> 00:57:34.330
But that view is not to
be found very often today.

00:57:34.330 --> 00:57:36.310
I mean, except for
some minor places,

00:57:36.310 --> 00:57:44.820
like Thailand and
the United Kingdom,

00:57:44.820 --> 00:57:51.130
this is a view that is done.

00:57:51.130 --> 00:57:54.820
Or consider chattel
slavery, the view

00:57:54.820 --> 00:57:57.670
that some people
are by right masters

00:57:57.670 --> 00:57:59.590
and some people are
by right slaves,

00:57:59.590 --> 00:58:01.750
and that the slave
holders could do

00:58:01.750 --> 00:58:03.880
what they wanted with
the slaves and break up

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:06.760
families, all the rest of that.

00:58:06.760 --> 00:58:10.900
This doctrine was vigorously
supported by Aristotle.

00:58:10.900 --> 00:58:15.580
It was considered to
be an obvious truth.

00:58:15.580 --> 00:58:18.760
Not only that, that it was
God given that that's the way

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:20.280
the world was to be arranged.

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:24.160
But there there's been a
stirring worldwide revolution.

00:58:24.160 --> 00:58:26.087
And there are very few
places on the earth

00:58:26.087 --> 00:58:27.670
in which chattel
slavery is considered

00:58:27.670 --> 00:58:31.896
acceptable or permissible or
socially respectable today.

00:58:31.896 --> 00:58:36.110
Well, the vested interests
in the divine right of kings

00:58:36.110 --> 00:58:40.840
and in slavery seem
to me far greater

00:58:40.840 --> 00:58:43.600
than the vested interests in
continuing the nuclear arms

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:46.360
race, although there certainly
are such vested interests.

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:50.380
And everybody on the
planet has something

00:58:50.380 --> 00:58:54.020
to gain from reversing
the nuclear arms race.

00:58:54.020 --> 00:58:57.670
And I think this is
something that can be done,

00:58:57.670 --> 00:59:00.820
but only if we understand
the nature of the issue

00:59:00.820 --> 00:59:06.370
and if we're willing to take
courageous personal steps

00:59:06.370 --> 00:59:09.100
to turn the situation around.

00:59:09.100 --> 00:59:11.750
Okay, I'm told I have time
only for one more question.

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:14.000
Please.

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:18.950
AUDIENCE: Since you [INAUDIBLE]
education in the media,

00:59:18.950 --> 00:59:23.405
[INAUDIBLE] show a
program that would wake up

00:59:23.405 --> 00:59:27.414
the world to all these issues?

00:59:27.414 --> 00:59:28.330
CARL SAGAN: Thank you.

00:59:28.330 --> 00:59:32.460
The questioner says, why
don't I make a television

00:59:32.460 --> 00:59:34.440
program to wake the
world up on these issues?

00:59:37.960 --> 00:59:40.440
I will take it under advisement.

00:59:44.040 --> 00:59:47.090
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

